---
// Faithful port of dashboard/src/pages/docs/DocsStripe.jsx — same sections in the same order
// (Who this is for -> key terms -> what you will set up -> API/webhook-recipe notice -> steps 1-2
// with both callouts -> how to verify -> common mistakes -> next step), text verbatim. Not
// reworded, for the reason batch 1 recorded: §29.1 puts website voice under
// docs/SourceTrack_GTM.md, and this is technical documentation that already reads correctly, so a
// port that rewrote it would be a rewrite wearing a port's name.
//
// This is the CUSTOMER-buyer webhook (api/routes/stripe-webhook.js, POST /:site_key — the
// revenue-by-source rail), NOT SourceTrack's own billing webhook (CLAUDE.md §7). The page's
// endpoint, event list, metadata key order, and signing-secret instructions all describe that
// route, and are carried over unchanged rather than restated — restating them is exactly how the
// two webhooks get conflated.
//
// The amber "API and webhook integration recipe" notice, the accepted-metadata-key order, the
// refunds-as-negative-conversions callout, and all three Common Mistakes are carried over
// verbatim. They are §6 honesty boundaries, not decoration: they are what stops a reader
// believing there is a Stripe marketplace plugin, that a custom metadata.st_aid key will be read,
// or that a missing signing secret fails silently rather than returning 400.
//
// DELIBERATE DIFFERENCES FROM THE SOURCE:
//   1. DocsCodeBlock / DocsCallout are React components (copy-to-clipboard, site-key
//      substitution). No Astro equivalent exists, so their CHROME is re-expressed with the
//      inline markup this directory already uses. Text inside is unchanged.
//   2. <Link to="/developers/offline-conversions"> becomes a plain href — the same path exists on
//      this site, so the target is unchanged, only the mechanism.
//
// The Checkout Session example lives in a frontmatter const: inlined in an .astro template its
// object braces would be parsed as JS expressions rather than rendered as copyable code.
//
// Canonical is deliberately NOT set: Base.astro derives `https://www.sourcetrack.ai{pathname}`,
// which is exactly the target DocsStripe.jsx's canonical is repointed to in this same change.
import Base from "@/layouts/Base.astro";

const breadcrumbSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
  "itemListElement": [
    { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 1, "name": "Home", "item": "https://www.sourcetrack.ai" },
    { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 2, "name": "Docs", "item": "https://www.sourcetrack.ai/docs" },
    { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 3, "name": "Stripe Webhook / API Revenue Attribution Recipe", "item": "https://www.sourcetrack.ai/docs/stripe" }
  ]
};

const CHECKOUT_SESSION_SNIPPET = `// Example of passing st_aid in Stripe Checkout Session creation (Node.js backend)
const session = await stripe.checkout.sessions.create({
  payment_method_types: ['card'],
  line_items: [{ price: 'price_H5gg...', quantity: 1 }],
  mode: 'subscription',
  success_url: 'https://yoursite.com/success',
  cancel_url: 'https://yoursite.com/cancel',

  // Method A: Pass directly as client_reference_id (Recommended)
  client_reference_id: req.body.st_aid,

  // Method B: Pass inside metadata object
  metadata: {
    anonymous_id: req.body.st_aid
  }
});`;
---

<Base
  title="Stripe Webhook/API Revenue Attribution Recipe | SourceTrack Docs"
  description="Stitch Stripe billing and subscription events with acquisition sources. Metadata structures and webhook configurations."
  structuredData={[breadcrumbSchema]}
>
  <section class="py-16 bg-[#0E1111] text-white">
    <div class="max-w-[800px] mx-auto px-4 space-y-8">
      <div class="space-y-4">
        <a href="/docs" class="text-xs uppercase tracking-widest font-extrabold text-[#C8F000]">&larr; Back to Docs</a>
        <h1 class="text-4xl font-black text-white tracking-tight">Stripe Webhook / API Revenue Attribution Recipe</h1>
        <p class="text-gray-400 text-lg leading-relaxed">
          Stitch subscription and payment events with marketing campaigns using Stripe metadata and webhook
          listener configurations.
        </p>
      </div>

      <section class="space-y-2">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-extrabold text-white border-b border-[#2A2C30] pb-2">Who This Is For</h2>
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
          This recipe is for developers and founders running SaaS or subscription-based sites on Stripe who
          want to link user checkouts, paid subscriptions, and order amounts back to marketing channels
          (like Google Ads clicks or organic referrers).
        </p>
      </section>

      <div class="bg-[#101A24] border border-[#1E3A5F] rounded-2xl p-5">
        <h4 class="font-extrabold text-[#7CC4FF] mb-2 text-sm">Key Terms Defined:</h4>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1 text-xs text-[#A8CFF0] leading-relaxed">
          <li><strong>Webhook</strong> &mdash; a server-to-server HTTP message sent by Stripe to SourceTrack whenever a billing event occurs.</li>
          <li><strong>HMAC verification</strong> &mdash; a way to cryptographically confirm that the webhook payload was sent by Stripe and not modified in transit.</li>
          <li><strong>Checkout Session</strong> &mdash; Stripe's system for collecting payment details and managing customer transactions.</li>
          <li><strong>Visitor ID (st_aid)</strong> &mdash; the anonymous tracking identifier that maps the customer's click journey.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <section class="space-y-2">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-extrabold text-white border-b border-[#2A2C30] pb-2">What You Will Set Up</h2>
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
          You will configure your backend server code to retrieve the visitor ID from local storage (or a
          browser cookie) and pass it into Stripe's checkout session metadata. You will then set up a
          webhook endpoint inside Stripe to forward completed checkout session events to SourceTrack.
        </p>
      </section>

      <div class="bg-amber-400/5 border border-amber-400/25 rounded-2xl p-5 text-sm text-amber-300 leading-relaxed">
        <strong>Integration notice:</strong> This setup is an API and webhook integration recipe.
        SourceTrack does not provide an official Stripe Dashboard extension or marketplace plugin. You must
        configure metadata forwarding in your billing system code and set up webhooks manually in your
        Stripe Dashboard.
      </div>

      <section class="space-y-6">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-extrabold text-white border-b border-[#2A2C30] pb-2">Steps: Stripe Integration</h2>

        <div class="space-y-4">
          <h3 class="text-sm font-extrabold text-white">Step 1: Forward Visitor ID in Stripe Metadata</h3>
          <p class="text-sm text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
            When creating a Stripe Checkout Session on your backend, read the visitor ID (stored in the
            browser as <code class="text-[#C8F000]">st_aid</code>) from the client's request payload and
            pass it as <code class="text-[#C8F000]">client_reference_id</code> or inside the
            <code class="text-[#C8F000]">metadata</code> block as
            <code class="text-[#C8F000]">anonymous_id</code> or <code class="text-[#C8F000]">visitor_id</code>.
          </p>
          <pre class="mt-2 bg-[#0F1012] p-4 rounded-xl text-xs text-gray-300 overflow-x-auto border border-[#2A2C30]"><code>{CHECKOUT_SESSION_SNIPPET}</code></pre>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">
            * Note: The SourceTrack webhook listener checks
            <code class="text-[#C8F000]">client_reference_id</code>,
            <code class="text-[#C8F000]">metadata.anonymous_id</code>,
            <code class="text-[#C8F000]">metadata.visitor_id</code>,
            <code class="text-[#C8F000]">metadata.sourcetrack_user_id</code>, and
            <code class="text-[#C8F000]">metadata.site_user_id</code> in that order.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="space-y-4 pt-4 border-t border-[#2A2C30]">
          <h3 class="text-sm font-extrabold text-white">Step 2: Configure Stripe Webhook</h3>
          <p class="text-sm text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
            Route event messages from Stripe directly to the SourceTrack ingestion URL:
          </p>
          <ol class="list-decimal pl-5 space-y-2 text-sm text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
            <li>Log in to your <strong>Stripe Dashboard</strong> and navigate to <strong>Developers &rarr; Webhooks</strong>.</li>
            <li>Click <strong>Add Endpoint</strong>.</li>
            <li>
              Enter your customized webhook endpoint URL:
              <div class="my-1.5 font-mono text-xs p-2 bg-[#0F1012] border border-[#2A2C30] rounded overflow-x-auto">
                https://api.srctk.com/api/webhooks/stripe/YOUR_SITE_KEY
              </div>
              Replace <code class="text-[#C8F000]">YOUR_SITE_KEY</code> with the Site Key found under
              settings in your dashboard.
            </li>
            <li>
              Under <strong>Select events to listen to</strong>, add:
              <ul class="list-disc pl-5 mt-1">
                <li><code class="text-[#C8F000]">checkout.session.completed</code></li>
                <li><code class="text-[#C8F000]">refund.created</code></li>
              </ul>
            </li>
            <li>Click <strong>Add Endpoint</strong>.</li>
          </ol>
          <div class="bg-[#101A24] border border-[#1E3A5F] rounded-2xl p-5 text-sm text-[#A8CFF0] leading-relaxed">
            Refunds are recorded as negative conversions and automatically net your revenue by source
            &mdash; subscribe to <code class="text-[#C8F000]">refund.created</code>.
          </div>
          <div class="bg-[#101A24] border border-[#1E3A5F] rounded-2xl p-5 text-sm text-[#A8CFF0] leading-relaxed">
            To secure your webhook, save the Stripe Webhook signing secret (starts with
            <code class="text-[#C8F000]">whsec_</code>) in your SourceTrack dashboard under
            <strong>Integrations &rarr; Stripe settings</strong>. This allows SourceTrack to perform
            cryptographic HMAC verification of incoming payloads.
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-extrabold text-white border-b border-[#2A2C30] pb-2">How to Verify It Worked</h2>
        <ol class="list-decimal pl-5 space-y-3 text-sm text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
          <li>Run a payment check through Stripe in <strong>Test Mode</strong>.</li>
          <li>In your Stripe Dashboard under <strong>Developers &rarr; Webhooks</strong>, inspect the event logs for <code class="text-[#C8F000]">checkout.session.completed</code>.</li>
          <li>Verify that the webhook request returned a HTTP <code class="text-[#C8F000]">200 OK</code> response.</li>
          <li>Verify that the transaction data (revenue and site metadata) appears attributed in the <strong>Event Debugger</strong> of your SourceTrack dashboard.</li>
        </ol>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-extrabold text-white border-b border-[#2A2C30] pb-2">Common Mistakes</h2>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2 text-sm text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
          <li>
            <strong>Incorrect Webhook Event Types:</strong> Stripe triggers a variety of events like
            <code class="text-[#C8F000]">payment_intent.succeeded</code>. SourceTrack parses
            <code class="text-[#C8F000]">checkout.session.completed</code>,
            <code class="text-[#C8F000]">refund.created</code>, and subscription lifecycle events; other
            events are safely ignored.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Unmatched Metadata Key:</strong> If you use custom names like
            <code class="text-[#C8F000]">metadata.st_aid</code>, SourceTrack's engine will not process it.
            Stick to <code class="text-[#C8F000]">client_reference_id</code> or
            <code class="text-[#C8F000]">metadata.anonymous_id</code>.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Missing Stripe Signing Secret:</strong> If you omit the signing secret under Settings,
            webhook payload signature checks will fail, returning a
            <code class="text-[#C8F000]">400 Bad Request</code>.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-2">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-extrabold text-white border-b border-[#2A2C30] pb-2">Next Step</h2>
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
          Stripe setup is complete! Go to the
          <a href="/developers/offline-conversions" class="text-[#C8F000] font-bold hover:underline">Offline Conversions API</a>
          to learn how to register custom offline sales or updates directly from your server.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
